    Convenient for a POC, catastrophic if it ever reaches a deployed environment.
  - [1mHand-rolled crypto instead of audited libraries.[0m Real HS256 and scrypt from [38;5;153mnode:crypto[39m, but the JWT parsing and
    comparison logic is mine, not [38;5;153mjsonwebtoken[39m's. Right tradeoff for a zero-dependency POC; not something to ship.
  - [1mPassword reset grants account takeover.[0m The reset token printed to stdout is a full credential — anyone reading the
    logs can seize any account. Fine for a mock mailer, but it means logs are secrets here.

  You told me not to ask questions, and I won't ask any about the design — the assumptions above are mine to make. This
  one I can't skip: the ladder says a security-posture change needs an explicit yes before the first edit, and it says
  the original request doesn't count as that yes.

  Say "yes" (or "go") and I'll write the spec, the implementation plan, and build it. If you'd rather change any of
  those four points — shorter expiry, required secret, real libraries — say so in the same breath and I'll fold it in.

